B.BI=s(["\u043d\u044f\u0434\u0437\u0435\u043b\u044f","\u043f\u0430\u043d\u044f\u0434\u0437\u0435\u043b\u0430\u043a","\u0430\u045e\u0442\u043e\u0440\u0430\u043a","\u0441\u0435\u0440\u0430\u0434\u0430","\u0447\u0430\u0446\u0432\u0435\u0440","\u043f\u044f\u0442\u043d\u0456\u0446\u0430","\u0441\u0443\u0431\u043e\u0442\u0430"],t.s)
B.aen=s(["Trigger, action, consequence","Cue, routine, reward","Stimulus, response, reinforcement","Beginning, middle, end"],t.s)
B.arP=new A.bR("What are the three components of the habit loop?",B.aen,1)
B.a96=s(["21 days","30 days","66 days","90 days"],t.s)
B.arq=new A.bR("How long does research suggest it takes to form a new habit?",B.a96,2)
B.ae9=s(["Why habits are impossible to change","Why breaking old habits is challenging","Why some people don't have habits","Why rewards don't matter"],t.s)
B.arl=new A.bR("What does neuroplasticity explain?",B.ae9,1)
B.a9B=s([B.arP,B.arq,B.arl],t.Q)
B.aDo=new A.f7("Understanding how habits form is crucial for personal development. Neuroscientists have discovered that our brains create powerful pathways through repetition. When we repeat an action, neural connections strengthen, making the behavior automatic over time. This process explains why breaking old habits can be challenging - the neural pathways are deeply established. However, the brain's neuroplasticity means we can always form new pathways and create healthier habits. Research suggests it takes an average of sixty-six days to establish a new routine, though this varies significantly between individuals and behaviors.",B.a9B)
B.a6F=s(["They can perform actual surgeries remotely","They practice complex procedures through simulations","They can diagnose patients more quickly","They can design medical equipment"],t.s)
B.ar7=new A.bR("How do surgeons benefit from Virtual Reality?",B.a6F,1)
B.ab3=s(["It replaces traditional textbooks entirely","It allows safe exploration of dangerous or impossible scenarios","It automatically grades student assignments","It reduces the cost of school supplies"],t.s)
B.arb=new A.bR("What educational benefit does VR provide?",B.ab3,1)
B.a8m=s(["To construct buildings automatically","To show clients building designs before construction","To calculate construction costs","To design building materials"],t.s)
B.ard=new A.bR("How do architects use Virtual Reality?",B.a8m,1)
B.a9P=s([B.ar7,B.arb,B.ard],t.Q)
B.aDt=new A.f7("Virtual Reality technology has transformed multiple industries in recent years. In medicine, surgeons use VR simulations to practice complex procedures before operating on actual patients. This training significantly reduces surgical errors and improves patient outcomes. Education has also embraced VR, allowing students to visit historical sites, explore the human body from inside, or conduct dangerous chemistry experiments safely. Architects utilize VR to walk clients through building designs before construction begins, saving time and resources. The entertainment industry continues to push VR boundaries with increasingly immersive gaming experiences that blur the line between virtual and reality.",B.a9P)
B.a7u=s(["Less than 1%","Approximately 10%","Approximately 25%","More than 50%"],t.s)
B.arH=new A.bR("What percentage of marine species do coral reefs support?",B.a7u,2)
B.a72=s(["Overfishing practices","Rising ocean temperatures","Ocean pollution","Storm damage"],t.s)
B.arp=new A.bR("What is the main cause of coral bleaching?",B.a72,1)
B.a9L=s(["Fresh water production","Coastal protection from storms","Oxygen generation for the atmosphere","Food production for land animals"],t.s)
B.arS=new A.bR("What essential service do coral reefs provide?",B.a9L,1)
B.acB=s([B.arH,B.arp,B.arS],t.Q)
B.aDl=new A.f7("Coral reef ecosystems are among the most diverse and valuable ecosystems on Earth. These underwater structures support approximately twenty-five percent of all marine species despite covering less than one percent of the ocean floor. Coral reefs provide essential services including coastal protection from storms, tourism revenue, and habitat for countless marine organisms. However, these fragile ecosystems face severe threats from climate change, ocean acidification, pollution, and destructive fishing practices. Rising ocean temperatures cause coral bleaching events that can lead to mass die-offs. Conservation efforts worldwide focus on reducing local stressors and establishing marine protected areas to help reefs recover and adapt to changing conditions.",B.acB)
B.a3Y=s(["Evidence of current intelligent life","That Mars has always been completely dry","Evidence of past liquid water and potential life conditions","That Mars has a breathable atmosphere"],t.s)
B.aqM=new A.bR("What have Martian rovers discovered about Mars?",B.a3Y,2)
B.a5Z=s(["Finding a landing site","The long journey time and radiation exposure","Communicating with Earth","Finding water on the surface"],t.s)
B.arI=new A.bR("What is one of the main challenges for human Mars missions?",B.a5Z,1)
B.a5U=s(["Only NASA","Only private companies","NASA, SpaceX, and other space agencies","Only European space agencies"],t.s)
B.arB=new A.bR("Which organizations are planning human Mars missions?",B.a5U,2)
B.a5j=s([B.aqM,B.arI,B.arB],t.Q)
B.aDs=new A.f7("Mars exploration has captured human imagination for decades. Multiple robotic missions have successfully landed on the Red Planet, including rovers like Curiosity and Perseverance that have revolutionized our understanding of Martian geology and climate. These missions have discovered evidence that Mars once had liquid water on its surface and potentially conditions suitable for microbial life. Current plans include sending humans to Mars within the next two decades, with NASA, SpaceX, and other space agencies developing the necessary technology. Challenges include the long journey time, radiation exposure, and creating sustainable habitats. Scientists continue to study Mars to understand its history and assess its potential for future human colonization.",B.a5j)
B.a5k=s(["Physical recovery and muscle growth","Emotional regulation and creative thinking","Memory consolidation only","Immune system function"],t.s)
B.arc=new A.bR("What is REM sleep primarily associated with?",B.a5k,1)
B.a7L=s(["4-6 hours","5-7 hours","7-9 hours","10-12 hours"],t.s)
B.arO=new A.bR("How many hours of sleep do adults typically need?",B.a7L,2)
B.a6P=s(["Only temporary fatigue","Cardiovascular disease, diabetes, and depression","Only minor cognitive issues","No significant health effects"],t.s)
B.as0=new A.bR("What health problems are linked to chronic sleep deprivation?",B.a6P,1)
B.a54=s([B.arc,B.arO,B.as0],t.Q)
B.aDI=new A.f7("Sleep science has revealed that sleep is far more than just rest. During sleep, the brain performs essential functions including memory consolidation, emotional processing, and physical restoration. Sleep consists of multiple cycles, each containing different stages. Non-REM sleep includes deep sleep stages crucial for physical recovery and immune function. REM sleep, characterized by rapid eye movements and vivid dreaming, is essential for emotional regulation and creative thinking. Chronic sleep deprivation has been linked to numerous health problems including cardiovascular disease, diabetes, depression, and impaired cognitive function. Adults typically need seven to nine hours of quality sleep per night, though individual requirements vary based on genetics, age, and lifestyle factors.",B.a54)
B.abH=s(["Being the first library ever built","Housing hundreds of thousands of scrolls and scholars","Containing only religious texts","Being destroyed by natural disasters only"],t.s)
B.arg=new A.bR("What was the Library of Alexandria known for?",B.abH,1)
B.abL=s(["Only by natural disasters","Only by accidental fires","Through war, fire, or deliberate destruction","They were never destroyed"],t.s)
B.arJ=new A.bR("How were ancient libraries typically destroyed?",B.abL,2)
B.a4D=s(["Private book collections","Monastic scriptoriums and imperial libraries","Oral storytelling traditions","Government archives only"],t.s)
B.art=new A.bR("What continued the tradition of preserving knowledge after ancient libraries?",B.a4D,1)
B.a3_=s([B.arg,B.arJ,B.art],t.Q)
B.aDu=new A.f7("Ancient libraries were centers of knowledge and learning that played crucial roles in preserving human civilization. The Library of Alexandria in Egypt was perhaps the most famous, housing hundreds of thousands of scrolls and attracting scholars from across the known world. These libraries served as repositories for literature, scientific texts, historical records, and philosophical works. Many ancient libraries were destroyed through war, fire, or deliberate destruction, resulting in the loss of countless irreplaceable works. The tradition of collecting and preserving knowledge continued through monastic scriptoriums in medieval Europe and imperial libraries in Asia. Today's digital libraries continue this mission of making knowledge accessible to all, though they face new challenges in preservation and accessibility.",B.a3_)
B.aeC=s(["Solar panels and solar mirrors","Photovoltaic panels and concentrated solar power","Solar cells and solar batteries","Solar collectors and solar heaters"],t.s)
B.arf=new A.bR("What are the two main methods for harnessing solar energy?",B.aeC,1)
B.a5q=s(["Only government regulations","Climate change concerns, energy security, and declining costs","Only the desire for new technology","Only fossil fuel depletion"],t.s)
B.ar5=new A.bR("What is driving the transition to renewable energy?",B.a5q,1)
B.adH=s(["Finding locations for installation","Energy storage and the intermittent nature of some sources","Creating the energy","Transporting the equipment"],t.s)
B.arA=new A.bR("What is one of the main challenges for renewable energy?",B.adH,1)
B.a7g=s([B.arf,B.ar5,B.arA],t.Q)
B.aDw=new A.f7("Renewable energy sources are transforming how the world generates power. Solar energy harnesses sunlight through photovoltaic panels or concentrated solar power systems. Wind turbines convert kinetic energy from air movement into electricity. Both technologies have become increasingly efficient and cost-effective over recent decades. Hydropower remains one of the largest renewable sources globally, though large dams raise environmental concerns. Emerging technologies include tidal power, geothermal energy, and advanced biofuels. The transition to renewables is driven by climate change concerns, energy security needs, and declining costs. However, challenges remain including energy storage, grid integration, and the intermittent nature of some renewable sources. Many countries have set ambitious targets for renewable energy adoption in coming decades.",B.a7g)
B.aeD=s(["An early digital photography method","A process producing detailed images on silver-plated copper","A color photography technique","A modern camera technology"],t.s)
B.aro=new A.bR("What was the daguerreotype process?",B.aeD,1)
B.abM=s(["It improved image quality dramatically","It made photography portable for the masses","It was the first color film","It was the first digital medium"],t.s)
B.aqP=new A.bR("What made roll film significant in photography history?",B.abM,1)
B.ac4=s(["It made photography more expensive","It eliminated the need for film and enabled instant review","It reduced image quality","It made cameras larger and heavier"],t.s)
B.aqN=new A.bR("How has digital technology changed photography?",B.ac4,1)
B.adl=s([B.aro,B.aqP,B.aqN],t.Q)
B.aDG=new A.f7("The evolution of photography spans nearly two centuries of technological innovation. The first permanent photograph was created in the early nineteenth century using a camera obscura and light-sensitive chemicals. The daguerreotype process, developed in France, produced detailed images on silver-plated copper. Later, wet plate collodion and dry plate processes improved accessibility and quality. The twentieth century brought roll film, making photography portable for the masses. Color photography emerged through various chemical processes before becoming standardized. Digital technology revolutionized photography in the late twentieth century, eliminating the need for film and enabling instant image review. Today, smartphone cameras have made photography ubiquitous, while professional equipment continues to push technical boundaries with higher resolution and improved low-light performance.",B.adl)
B.abv=s(["Training at maximum intensity every session","Gradually increasing intensity to stimulate adaptation","Training only when feeling motivated","Keeping training intensity constant"],t.s)
B.as_=new A.bR("What is the principle of progressive overload in athletic training?",B.abv,1)
B.a6B=s(["It allows athletes to rest completely","It enables the body to repair and strengthen","It prevents all injuries","It is only needed after competitions"],t.s)
B.arR=new A.bR("Why is recovery important in athletic training?",B.a6B,1)
B.ab0=s(["Only physical conditioning","Visualization, goal setting, and mindfulness","Only watching other athletes","Only increasing training volume"],t.s)
B.arQ=new A.bR("What mental training techniques help athletes manage pressure?",B.ab0,1)
B.ad7=s([B.as_,B.arR,B.arQ],t.Q)
B.aDp=new A.f7("Athletic performance science combines physiology, biomechanics, nutrition, and psychology to help athletes reach their potential. Training programs are designed based on the principle of progressive overload, gradually increasing intensity to stimulate adaptation. Recovery is equally important, incorporating sleep, nutrition, and active rest to allow the body to repair and strengthen. Sports nutrition focuses on timing of macronutrients around training sessions to optimize energy availability and muscle recovery. Mental training techniques including visualization, goal setting, and mindfulness help athletes maintain focus and manage pressure. Technology plays an increasing role, with wearable devices monitoring performance metrics and video analysis providing detailed feedback on technique. Understanding individual differences in genetics, physiology, and psychology allows for personalized training approaches.",B.ad7)
B.a5N=s(["They increase urban heat island effect","They reduce urban heat island effect and improve air quality","They increase water consumption","They require no maintenance"],t.s)
B.ar3=new A.bR("What is one environmental benefit of urban gardens?",B.a5N,1)
B.a2X=s(["They create community gathering spaces and strengthen social connections","They only provide decorative value","They increase property values only","They reduce social interactions"],t.s)
B.arn=new A.bR("How do urban gardens benefit communities?",B.a2X,1)
B.a9l=s(["No proven health benefits","Improved mental health and reduced stress","Only physical exercise benefits","Only improved respiratory health"],t.s)
B.arm=new A.bR("What health benefits are associated with urban green spaces?",B.a9l,1)
B.aar=s([B.ar3,B.arn,B.arm],t.Q)
B.aDv=new A.f7("Urban gardens are transforming cities by bringing green spaces into densely populated areas. These gardens can take many forms, from community plots tended by neighborhood residents to rooftop gardens on commercial buildings. Urban agriculture provides fresh produce to food deserts where access to healthy food is limited. Beyond food production, urban gardens offer numerous environmental benefits including reduced urban heat island effect, improved air quality, and stormwater management. They also create community gathering spaces that strengthen social connections and provide educational opportunities about food systems and nature. Studies have shown that exposure to green spaces in urban environments improves mental health and reduces stress. Cities worldwide are incorporating urban gardens into planning as part of sustainable development strategies.",B.aar)
